{:edb/db-before-t 1509 :edb/db-after-t 1510 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534822 50 #inst "2026-10-08T08:05:49.837Z" 13194139534822 true] [17592186096437 1100 "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $P(A\\cup B) = \\dfrac{4}{9}$, find $P(B)$." 13194139534822 false] [17592186096437 1100 "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $P(A\\cup B) = \\dfrac{4}{9},$ find $P(B).$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186096437 1145 "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪B) &= P(A) + P(B) \\\\\nP(B) &= P(A∪B) - P(A) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{9} - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 3}{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534822 false] [17592186096437 1145 "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B) = 0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(B) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-3}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186096438 1100 "Two cards are drawn from a standard ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/c1a5898916d3156aa9acb1884f2ab9fd149c10ca2149574a50fc6147586691e6.png)-card deck: the ace of hearts and the queen of hearts. These are set aside, and a third card is drawn from the remaining deck. What is the probability that the third drawn card is an ace or a heart?\n\n*Hint: A standard ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/5f378bdc2525ccdfd98ae36dc698e3bdf3f328a76d6841faf2198ec37b34db9a.png)-card deck has ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/d44ac804f533a60a0d252fbcddaec9693d8b989dc429bf03f3ef1f0587cf3453.png) suits with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/f3ae95c18ff411808c146e5d3bfc30f49380b8272511f1a90a3400209b2df340.png) cards in each suit.There is one ace in each suit.\n Hearts is one of the suits.*" 13194139534822 true] [17592186096438 1100 "Two cards are drawn from a standard $52$-card deck: the ace of hearts and the queen of hearts. These are set apart, and a third card is drawn from the remaining deck. What is the probability that the third drawn card is an ace or a heart?" 13194139534822 false] [17592186096438 1145 "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/15fa693fff5bc0eb2a8851089612b5a3fb969c3fd5fbc7c0821e58e52ff4260d.png) be the event that the third card is an ace, and let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/86056525c7e84fd98e233f12873f4b7bd6ed728224c7c1afe986743f6cda0aa0.png) be the event that the third card is a heart. Then the probability that the third card is an ace or a heart can be represented as ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/a143776333b936a72708b71c7deadb626e347a6c094630c9dbce894d550ed5bb.png)\n\nGiven that the ace of hearts is no longer in the deck, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/3397629141c3034fbd4b5cd5bd8c554676175a4b1e3f5eccd26cc5d66ee1bec8.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/d18d46bb8861930c4c3e602e4eef58228e745671a5445823a11d088e0a9e43a8.png) are mutually exclusive events, i.e., ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/37f1cba75404eaf490f8ae5f3de37c1da606e372b6b2e1f1c9c2c23a9ee0f82d.png)\n\nWe can find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/3abd50b1d24b25f8908834ec0b3ef94c630f1c8c5352c0528a381b4bddc30ed1.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/a73f6c30e5a00859bc7f04c5f08a59a978dcc714634187dd178a9fa2059d1578.png) by computing the necessary ratios. When the third card is drawn, there are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/c6852fb3b1ffe57a198559e8fb2ffc12bd834126e291117f7158395501b7dbc5.png) cards in the deck. Among the remaining ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/c6852fb3b1ffe57a198559e8fb2ffc12bd834126e291117f7158395501b7dbc5.png) cards, there are ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/97314fc826b205001aa477df5cccb4ceb42aff037d8ccf663a40341a70629d9f.png) hearts and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/6da8ea22dec2b00f0f9fa815118a83e3877bfdba5149ea487f242a1839878b0e.png) aces. Therefore:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/ecf686508e7fe0e4454085c9af1c06ce613ebf9dc1ef4c5bc2f0f93a146a8a23.png)\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041669/assets/456f4dde4ad17f8738a277bb3a6eb139db35bb20aaa85dba794f2f1730bd1495.png)" 13194139534822 true] [17592186096438 1145 "Let $A$ be the event that the third card is an ace, and let $H$ be the event that the third card is a heart. Then the probability that the third card is an ace or a heart can be represented as $P(A\\cup H)$.\n\nGiven that the ace of hearts is no longer in the deck, $A$ and $H$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e.,\n\n$$\nP(A \\cap H) = 0\n$$\n\nWe can find $P(A)$ and $P(H)$ by computing the necessary ratios. When the third card is drawn, there are $50$ cards in the deck. Among the remaining $50$ cards, there are $13-2=11$ hearts and $4-1=3$ aces. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= \\frac{3}{50} \\\\\nP(H) &= \\frac{11}{50}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪H) &= P(A) + P(H) - P(A∩H) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{50} + \\frac{11}{50} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{14}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534822 false] [17592186096446 1100 "Given $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3}$, $P(B)=\\dfrac{1}{4}$, and $P(A\\cup B)=\\dfrac{7}{12}$, determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139534822 false] [17592186096446 1100 "Given $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3},$ $P(B)=\\dfrac{1}{4},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\dfrac{7}{12},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139534822 true] [17592186096446 1145 "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = 0\n$$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪B) &= P(A) + P(B) - P(A∩B) \\\\\n\\frac{7}{12} &= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4} - P(A∩B) \\\\\nP(A∩B) &= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{7}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 + 3 - 7}{12} \\\\\n&= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139534822 false] [17592186096446 1145 "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B) = 0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{7}{12} & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{7}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+3-7}{12} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139534822 true] [17592186096644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312454 13194139534822 true] [17592186096644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312482 13194139534822 true] [17592186096644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312524 13194139534822 true] [17592186096644 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312531 13194139534822 true] [17592186096645 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312461 13194139534822 true] [17592186096645 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312468 13194139534822 true] [17592186096645 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312489 13194139534822 true] [17592186096645 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312496 13194139534822 true] [17592186096648 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312447 13194139534822 true] [17592186096648 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312475 13194139534822 true] [17592186096648 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312503 13194139534822 true] [17592186096648 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312510 13194139534822 true] [17592186096648 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312517 13194139534822 true] [17592186096975 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534822 true] [17592186096975 1100 "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A) = \\frac{2}{7}$ and $P(A∪B) = \\frac{3}{4}$, find $P(B)$." 13194139534822 false] [17592186096975 1100 "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A)=\\frac{2}{7}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{3}{4},$ find $P(B).$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186096975 1145 "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(B) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{21-8}{28} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{28}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186097241 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534822 true] [17592186097241 1145 "Let $R$ be the event that the name starts with an \"R,\" and let $E$ be the event that the name starts with an \"E.\" Then the probability that the name will start with an \"R\" or an \"E\" is given by $P(R\\cup E).$\n\nGiven that only one letter can be picked as the first letter, $R$ and $E$ are mutually exclusive events. i.e. $P(R\\cap E)=0.$\n\nThere are $26$ letters in the alphabet, and each letter has an equal chance of being randomly picked ($1$ out of $26$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =\\frac{1}{26}, \\\\ P(E) & =\\frac{1}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R\\cup E) & =P(R)+P(E)-P(R\\cap E) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}+\\frac{1}{26}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{26} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312447 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312448 13194139534822 true] [17592186312447 1089 "q-120513" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312447 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534822 true] [17592186312447 1100 "Given $P(A)=\\frac{3}{8},$ $P(B)=\\frac{1}{3},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{17}{24},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139534822 true] [17592186312447 1113 #uuid "38965b82-21b5-5bb0-b47b-24c8c7b13476" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312447 1145 "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{17}{24} & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{1}{3}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{17}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+8-17}{24} \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139534822 true] [17592186312448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312449 13194139534822 true] [17592186312448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312450 13194139534822 true] [17592186312448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312451 13194139534822 true] [17592186312448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312452 13194139534822 true] [17592186312448 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312448 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534822 true] [17592186312448 1041 "selection" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312448 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312450 13194139534822 true] [17592186312448 1110 #uuid "da3b0e32-52dd-575d-b4ac-e22d9ee651f9" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312449 1034 "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)\\ne P(A\\cup B)$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312449 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534822 true] [17592186312449 1111 #uuid "173d63d0-edfb-567b-b629-c32983d51142" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312450 1034 "$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)=0$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312450 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534822 true] [17592186312450 1111 #uuid "1bd80d9d-fca1-528e-ba1c-ce0ebadb10bb" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312451 1034 "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)<P(A\\cup B)$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312451 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534822 true] [17592186312451 1111 #uuid "a9f4a75f-e2b5-58f9-84fe-0fb3d1b6eca7" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312452 1034 "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312452 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534822 true] [17592186312452 1111 #uuid "6cbd41b3-74c6-5290-bad3-f2db34023479" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312453 1034 "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\ne P(B)$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312453 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534822 true] [17592186312453 1111 #uuid "26c7e901-2298-59dc-b67b-68e0a0b592e3" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312454 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312455 13194139534822 true] [17592186312454 1089 "q-120524" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312454 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534822 true] [17592186312454 1100 "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(B)=\\frac{8}{21}$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{3}{4},$ find $P(A).$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312454 1113 #uuid "3c835ba5-746a-5d4a-a539-db7fadba80c0" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312454 1145 "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(A) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(B) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{8}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{63-32}{84} \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{84}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312455 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312456 13194139534822 true] [17592186312455 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312457 13194139534822 true] [17592186312455 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312458 13194139534822 true] [17592186312455 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312459 13194139534822 true] [17592186312455 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312460 13194139534822 true] [17592186312455 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534822 true] [17592186312455 1041 "selection" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312455 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312457 13194139534822 true] [17592186312455 1110 #uuid "ebd7b59b-37dd-57cc-90c6-2c37218a41da" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312456 1034 "\\frac{13}{84}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312456 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312456 1111 #uuid "f4933e66-9c41-5512-9652-45ef2de0ba94" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312457 1034 "\\frac{31}{84}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312457 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312457 1111 #uuid "a3f4f2fa-5d63-56a5-803c-4ef9e31d6943" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312458 1034 "\\frac{23}{84}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312458 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312458 1111 #uuid "aed63c87-db80-5143-8c53-6d84b4dcbef8" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312459 1034 "\\frac{1}{7}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312459 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312459 1111 #uuid "815758e3-7fc8-55e5-baee-9e48553fe7ae" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312460 1034 "\\frac{3}{8}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312460 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312460 1111 #uuid "fb5f7d66-f3e5-5df4-9f95-0ebe83016b8b" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312461 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312462 13194139534822 true] [17592186312461 1089 "q-120526" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312461 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534822 true] [17592186312461 1100 "A number is picked from $10$ to $19.$ What is the probability of picking an even number or a prime number?" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312461 1113 #uuid "1db7a6ec-d7f9-50c5-b3f9-3a121c39ded3" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312461 1145 "Let $E$ be the event that the picked number is an even number, and let $R$ be the event that the picked number is a prime number. Then the probability that the selected number is an even number or a prime number is $P(E\\cup R).$\n\nGiven that all prime numbers between $10$ and $19$ are odd, $E$ and $R$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(E\\cap R)=0.$\n\nWe can find $P(E)$ and $P(R)$ by computing the necessary ratios. There are $10$ numbers from  $10$ to $19$, of which  $5$ are even, and $4$ are prime.   Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E) & =\\frac{5}{10} \\\\ P(R) & =\\frac{4}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E\\cup R) & =P(E)+P(R)-P(E\\cap R) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{10}+\\frac{4}{10}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312462 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312463 13194139534822 true] [17592186312462 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312464 13194139534822 true] [17592186312462 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312465 13194139534822 true] [17592186312462 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312466 13194139534822 true] [17592186312462 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312467 13194139534822 true] [17592186312462 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534822 true] [17592186312462 1041 "selection" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312462 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312463 13194139534822 true] [17592186312462 1110 #uuid "66c06ba9-bee7-5cd5-abe0-ed4fe50771c9" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312463 1034 "\\frac{9}{10}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312463 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312463 1111 #uuid "99a0c687-95d8-5749-bf9e-2f9b63990362" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312464 1034 "\\frac{3}{10}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312464 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312464 1111 #uuid "3737965e-5af6-5c5d-b9a1-50982b7e5dae" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312465 1034 "\\frac{2}{5}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312465 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312465 1111 #uuid "35c2cf11-17d7-5c10-87ae-5e0a09d47cc2" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312466 1034 "\\frac{3}{5}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312466 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312466 1111 #uuid "5dbef13e-6dd1-5c06-b65f-4371e3c5491a" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312467 1034 "\\frac{7}{10}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312467 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312467 1111 #uuid "8b8fc34b-21b2-5295-be25-cccf6cde47ef" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312468 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312469 13194139534822 true] [17592186312468 1089 "q-120545" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312468 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534822 true] [17592186312468 1100 "On a shelf, there are $11$ novels and $9$ science books. $1$ of the novels and $3$ of the science books are illustrated. The illustrated novel is taken from the shelf and set apart. If a single book is selected at random, what is the probability that it is a novel or an illustrated book?" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312468 1113 #uuid "e9f5f88f-94fe-59a3-858c-68b6a4ea8014" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312468 1145 "Let $I$ be the event that the selected book is an illustrated book, and let $N$ be the event that it is a novel. Then the probability that the selected book is a novel or an illustrated book is given by $P(I\\cup N).$\n\nSince the only illustrated novel was removed, there are no novels left on the shelf that are also illustrated. Therefore, $I$ and $N$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(I\\cap N)=0.$\n\nSince the illustrated novel was set apart, there are $10+9=19$ books remaining, of which $10$ are novels and $3$ are illustrated. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(I) & =\\frac{3}{19}, \\\\ P(N) & =\\frac{10}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(I\\cup N) & =P(I)+P(N)-P(I\\cap N) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{19}+\\frac{10}{19}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3+10}{19} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312470 13194139534822 true] [17592186312469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312471 13194139534822 true] [17592186312469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312472 13194139534822 true] [17592186312469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312473 13194139534822 true] [17592186312469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312474 13194139534822 true] [17592186312469 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534822 true] [17592186312469 1041 "selection" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312469 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312472 13194139534822 true] [17592186312469 1110 #uuid "bf423359-8d32-5275-ba24-80d4806e070a" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312470 1034 "\\frac{3}{10}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312470 1111 #uuid "cb7efbc7-86e4-51d5-a953-2439a4a18d19" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312471 1034 "\\frac{1}{10}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312471 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312471 1111 #uuid "69e0b292-fe54-54c9-bc4a-4aed2502b682" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312472 1034 "\\frac{13}{19}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312472 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312472 1111 #uuid "12bfca1f-8635-5a87-82b6-b210263fcc07" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312473 1034 "\\frac{2}{19}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312473 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312473 1111 #uuid "723c0850-fa31-5c6c-b242-e21ed2e6de9b" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312474 1034 "\\frac{1}{20}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312474 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312474 1111 #uuid "ccf521c5-9549-5401-bf47-24f4b8a631f9" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312475 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312476 13194139534822 true] [17592186312475 1089 "q-12549" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312475 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534822 true] [17592186312475 1100 "Given $P(A)=0.25$, $P(B)=0.75$, $P(A\\cup B)=0.8$, determine whether  $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." 13194139534822 true] [17592186312475 1113 #uuid "d5829032-59a0-5111-b1d8-47c7a5a05911" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312475 1145 "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ 0.8 & =0.25+0.75-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =0.25+0.75-0.8 \\\\  & =0.2\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events." 13194139534822 true] [17592186312476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312477 13194139534822 true] [17592186312476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312478 13194139534822 true] [17592186312476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312479 13194139534822 true] [17592186312476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312480 13194139534822 true] [17592186312476 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312481 13194139534822 true] [17592186312476 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534822 true] [17592186312476 1041 "selection" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312476 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312480 13194139534822 true] [17592186312476 1110 #uuid "7b8c64ef-ccfe-5956-ab1b-e59eff2a7735" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312477 1034 "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)\\ne P(A\\cup B)$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312477 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534822 true] [17592186312477 1111 #uuid "0d97ad7d-a83a-5e49-99eb-f79949cb7f18" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312478 1034 "$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)=0$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312478 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534822 true] [17592186312478 1111 #uuid "b6936ea4-ebd6-55d8-be7d-4bf96e5680ed" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312479 1034 "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)<P(A\\cup B)$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312479 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534822 true] [17592186312479 1111 #uuid "e9ea7738-6e91-5073-804b-d7bcb8437d65" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312480 1034 "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312480 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534822 true] [17592186312480 1111 #uuid "e01531d9-b7a4-5078-b24a-aa6ab4cc8d27" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312481 1034 "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\ne P(B)$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312481 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534822 true] [17592186312481 1111 #uuid "a61445f1-8a68-5adb-9914-f0a89b37c06a" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312482 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312483 13194139534822 true] [17592186312482 1089 "q-12552" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312482 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534822 true] [17592186312482 1100 "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A)=0.5$ and $P(A\\cup B)=0.7,$ find $P(B).$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312482 1113 #uuid "2b1aa864-facc-5f33-ab7f-7efe7ce4a1fd" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312482 1145 "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(B) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(A) \\\\  & =0.7-0.5 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312484 13194139534822 true] [17592186312483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312485 13194139534822 true] [17592186312483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312486 13194139534822 true] [17592186312483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312487 13194139534822 true] [17592186312483 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312488 13194139534822 true] [17592186312483 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534822 true] [17592186312483 1041 "selection" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312483 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312484 13194139534822 true] [17592186312483 1110 #uuid "bc3933ad-5091-5d8f-a7ac-5c0f282452ef" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312484 1034 "0.2" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312484 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312484 1111 #uuid "5e0873c3-1a10-543f-85ab-8eebe87fcc3f" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312485 1034 "0.28" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312485 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312485 1111 #uuid "91893fc4-fd91-56ce-b44c-cede7f6c8404" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312486 1034 "0.3" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312486 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312486 1111 #uuid "976b1181-7a48-50a6-8bd4-8b8d94503cc6" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312487 1034 "0.8" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312487 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312487 1111 #uuid "125ca9ed-4c79-5d83-ba81-bae105b45e75" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312488 1034 "0.5" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312488 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312488 1111 #uuid "3a63241c-df4f-51fc-8b18-2296350882ac" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312489 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312490 13194139534822 true] [17592186312489 1089 "q-12554" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312489 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534822 true] [17592186312489 1100 "A card is drawn from a standard $52$-card deck. The drawn card is the Ace of Spades and it is set aside. A second card is drawn from the deck. What is the probability that the drawn card is an ace or a spade?\n\n*Hint: A standard $52$-card deck has $4$ suits with $13$ cards in each suit. Spades is one of the suits.*" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312489 1113 #uuid "ffaa74bf-70c5-5164-b2b4-295879e59db0" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312489 1145 "Let us call $A$ the event \"The second card drawn is an ace,\" and $S$ the event \"The second card drawn is a spade.\" The required probability is given by  $P(A\\cup S).$ \n\nGiven that the Ace of Spades is no longer in the deck, $A$ and $S$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(A\\cap S)=0.$\n\nSince the Ace of Spades was set aside, there are $51$ cards remaining,  of which $3$ are aces and $12$ are spades. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{3}{51}, \\\\ P(S) & =\\frac{12}{51}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup S) & =P(A)+P(S)-P(A\\cap S) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{51}+\\frac{12}{51}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3+12}{51} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{51} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312490 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312491 13194139534822 true] [17592186312490 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312492 13194139534822 true] [17592186312490 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312493 13194139534822 true] [17592186312490 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312494 13194139534822 true] [17592186312490 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312495 13194139534822 true] [17592186312490 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534822 true] [17592186312490 1041 "selection" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312490 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312493 13194139534822 true] [17592186312490 1110 #uuid "8be57220-224e-59b9-9572-6140d3506fa2" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312491 1034 "\\frac{14}{51}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312491 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312491 1111 #uuid "36f094bd-4db2-58ba-92bd-7856fecd955e" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312492 1034 "\\frac{17}{51}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312492 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312492 1111 #uuid "61018851-76ff-537b-84ec-0a0082791012" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312493 1034 "\\frac{5}{17}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312493 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312493 1111 #uuid "9e04b427-0c9d-5679-b48c-6e1ab9bbeb07" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312494 1034 "\\frac{17}{52}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312494 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312494 1111 #uuid "e672e55c-583b-594d-8997-3e2d055f54b2" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312495 1034 "\\frac{15}{52}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312495 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534822 true] [17592186312495 1111 #uuid "78f4c3bd-a10f-5ebd-b313-2b0c153883c9" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312496 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312497 13194139534822 true] [17592186312496 1089 "q-5616" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312496 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534822 true] [17592186312496 1100 "A game show uses a roulette wheel divided into $50$ panels. There are $15$ blue, $15$ green, $15$ yellow, $2$ black, and $3$ white panels randomly shuffled on the roulette wheel. What is the probability that the roulette wheel stops on a white or green panel?" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312496 1113 #uuid "d1ed0374-b4e0-5a94-b18c-7a2811425755" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312496 1145 "Let $W$ be the event that the roulette will stop on a white panel, and let $G$ be the event that the roulette will stop on a green panel. Then the probability that the roulette stops on a white or green panel given by $P(W\\cup G).$\n\nGiven that the roulette will stop at only one panel, the events $W$ and $G$  are mutually exclusive, i.e., $P(W\\cap G)=0.$ \n\nWe can find $P(W)$ and $P(G)$ by computing the necessary ratios. There are $50$ panels in the roulette. Of those panels, $3$ are white, and $15$ are green. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =\\frac{3}{50}. \\\\ P(G) & =\\frac{15}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W\\cup G) & =P(W)+P(G)-P(W\\cap G) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{50}+\\frac{15}{50}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312497 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312498 13194139534822 true] [17592186312497 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312499 13194139534822 true] [17592186312497 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312500 13194139534822 true] [17592186312497 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312501 13194139534822 true] [17592186312497 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312502 13194139534822 true] [17592186312497 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534822 true] [17592186312497 1041 "selection" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312497 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312500 13194139534822 true] [17592186312497 1110 #uuid "e8fd6cc0-5195-5938-bda3-53708a6bd0e2" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312498 1034 "\\frac{6}{25}" 13194139534822 true] [17592186312498 1103 #edb/ref 1759